---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-validator">
  <DevFormatJson client:load mode="validate" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Reading the error message</h2>
    <p>
      JSON is a tiny grammar, so a failure always has one of a few causes. The JSON validator shows
      what it expected at that point, what it found, and the line and column. Then it shows the bad
      line with a caret under the exact character. That is more than <code>JSON.parse</code> gives,
      and it is the same in all browsers, which the built-in messages are not.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What you wrote</th><th>Why it fails</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": 1,&#125;</code></td><td>Trailing comma before the closing brace</td><td>Delete the comma</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;'a': 1&#125;</code></td><td>Single quotes are not JSON strings</td><td>Use double quotes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;a: 1&#125;</code></td><td>Unquoted key</td><td>Quote every key</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": 01&#125;</code></td><td>Leading zeros are not valid numbers</td><td>Write <code>1</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": 1.&#125;</code></td><td>A decimal point needs a digit after it</td><td>Write <code>1.0</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": NaN&#125;</code></td><td>NaN and Infinity are JavaScript, not JSON</td><td>Use <code>null</code> or a string</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>// note</code></td><td>JSON has no comments</td><td>Strip them, or use JSONC in your own tooling</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": "b<br />c"&#125;</code></td><td>A raw line break inside a string</td><td>Escape it as <code>\n</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>"C:\path"</code></td><td>A lone backslash starts an escape</td><td>Double it: <code>"C:\\path"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starts with <code>&lt;</code></td><td>An HTML error page arrived instead of JSON</td><td>Check the request, not the JSON</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What the one-click repair changes</h2>
    <p>
      The repair pass is one left-to-right rewrite that knows about strings: text between a pair of
      quotes is copied as it is, so a comma inside <code>"Doe, Jane"</code> is never read as a trailing
      comma. The tool counts each category before you apply it, so you see exactly what will change:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A byte-order mark before the first character (invisible, and a very common cause of &ldquo;unexpected token&rdquo; at position 0).</li>
      <li>Single-quoted strings converted to double quotes, with any internal double quotes escaped.</li>
      <li>Typographic &ldquo;smart quotes&rdquo; from a word processor or a chat app replaced with straight ones.</li>
      <li><code>//</code>, <code>#</code> and <code>/* … */</code> comments removed.</li>
      <li>Trailing commas before <code>&#125;</code> or <code>]</code> removed.</li>
      <li><code>NaN</code>, <code>Infinity</code> and <code>undefined</code> replaced with <code>null</code>.</li>
      <li>Bare object keys wrapped in double quotes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Repair changes your input in place, and validation runs again at once. If the result is still
      invalid, the remaining error is structural (an unclosed bracket, or two documents joined
      together), and you must fix it by hand.
    </p>

    <h2>Two warnings that appear on valid documents</h2>
    <p>
      Duplicate keys: <code>&#123;"a": 1, "a": 2&#125;</code> is legal JSON. All mainstream parsers keep
      the last occurrence and discard the first without a warning. In a config file that is an override
      you did not intend. In an API payload it usually means two code paths wrote the same field. The
      validator shows the key and the line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unsafe integers: JavaScript numbers are IEEE-754 doubles, so an integer above
      9,007,199,254,740,991 loses precision. A 19-digit Snowflake or Twitter-style ID rounds to a
      different number when it is parsed, without a warning, in both directions. The standard fix is to
      send large identifiers as strings.
    </p>

    <h2>Why local validation matters</h2>
    <p>
      JSON that you validate is usually a payload that does not work: a real request or response, with
      bearer tokens, customer records, internal identifiers and webhook secrets. In 2025, about 80,000
      saved snippets were recovered from public formatter sites, many with live cloud credentials. This
      page parses in your tab. The panel under the tool counts the network requests the page makes, so
      you can confirm the count is zero.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
